      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|=$",
      "worked_solution": "The $1$rst row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
